---
import HardwareNote from "../../layouts/HardwareNote.astro";
import HwSection from "../../components/HwSection.astro";
import HwFigure from "../../components/HwFigure.astro";
import HwNote from "../../components/HwNote.astro";

// The committed init sequence, command by command, in order. `bytes` is the
// payload length including the DCS command byte -- the third header byte.
// Transcribed from rm69a10_568x1232_dsi_send() and cross-checked against
// nix/dts/display-rm69a10-568x1232.dtsi.
const initSeq = [
  { cmd: "b9", bytes: 4, what: "extension command set" },
  { cmd: "d2", bytes: 2, what: "page select" },
  { cmd: "b1", bytes: 16, what: "power control" },
  { cmd: "b2", bytes: 16, what: "display resolution and porches" },
  { cmd: "b4", bytes: 45, what: "timing / blanking block" },
  { cmd: "d3", bytes: 34, what: "gate timing" },
  { cmd: "d5", bytes: 33, what: "forward gate mapping" },
  { cmd: "d6", bytes: 33, what: "reverse gate mapping" },
  { cmd: "d8", bytes: 17, what: "gate-on/off waveform" },
  { cmd: "bd", bytes: 2, what: "bank 1" },
  { cmd: "d8", bytes: 17, what: "gate-on/off waveform, bank 1" },
  { cmd: "bd", bytes: 2, what: "bank 2" },
  { cmd: "d8", bytes: 9, what: "gate-on/off waveform, bank 2" },
  { cmd: "bd", bytes: 2, what: "back to bank 0" },
  { cmd: "b6", bytes: 3, what: "VCOM" },
  { cmd: "cc", bytes: 2, what: "panel orientation — 0x04 is unmirrored" },
  { cmd: "c6", bytes: 3, what: "abnormal-power detection" },
  { cmd: "e0", bytes: 55, what: "gamma table" },
  { cmd: "11", bytes: 1, what: "exit sleep mode" },
  { cmd: "29", bytes: 1, what: "set display on" },
];
const payloadTotal = initSeq.reduce((n, c) => n + c.bytes, 0);
const propertyBytes = payloadTotal + initSeq.length * 3;

const dtsi = `<i>// nix/dts/display-rm69a10-568x1232.dtsi (abridged: the 20-command blob
// is shown with its four largest payloads elided for width)</i>

&amp;vo {
    <b>vth_line = &lt;9&gt;;</b>            <i>// intr_line from the vendor connector table; ST7701 uses 10</i>
};

&amp;dsi {
    ports {
        <b>port@1 {</b>
            <b>reg = &lt;1&gt;;</b>
            <b>dsi_out_rm69a10: endpoint { remote-endpoint = &lt;&amp;rm69a10_in&gt;; };</b>
        <b>};</b>
    };

    <b>lcd: panel@0 {</b>                <i>// a DSI child, and labelled so the board can reach it</i>
        <b>compatible = "canaan,universal";</b>
        <b>reg = &lt;0&gt;;</b>

        <i>// DIVERGENCE: the reference puts PIXEL COUNTS in these. These are real
        // millimetres, computed from the stated 4.1" diagonal — not measured.</i>
        <b>panel-width-mm  = &lt;44&gt;;</b>
        <b>panel-height-mm = &lt;95&gt;;</b>

        <b>panel-dsi-lane  = &lt;2&gt;;</b>

        <i>// framing: data_type, delay_ms, payload_length, payload…
        // delay is a u8 of MILLIseconds. The vendor waits 300 us after 0x11
        // and 100 us after 0x29; neither is expressible, so both become 1 ms,
        // which is longer than the vendor waits.</i>
        <b>panel-init-sequence = [</b>
            <b>39 00 04 b9 ff 83 99</b>
            <b>15 00 02 d2 aa</b>
            <b>39 00 10 b1 02 04 71 91 01 32 33 11 11 ab 4d 56 73 02 02</b>
            <b>39 00 10 b2 00 80 80 ae 05 07 5a 11 00 00 10 1e 70 03 d4</b>
            <b>39 00 2d b4 00 ff 02 c0 02 c0 …</b>          <i>45 bytes</i>
            <b>39 00 22 d3 00 00 00 00 00 00 …</b>          <i>34 bytes</i>
            <b>39 00 21 d5 18 18 19 19 18 18 …</b>          <i>33 bytes</i>
            <b>39 00 21 d6 18 18 19 19 40 40 …</b>          <i>33 bytes</i>
            <b>39 00 11 d8 a2 aa 02 a0 a2 a8 02 a0 b0 00 00 00 b0 00 00 00</b>
            <b>15 00 02 bd 01</b>
            <b>39 00 11 d8 b0 00 00 00 b0 00 00 00 e2 aa 03 f0 e2 aa 03 f0</b>
            <b>15 00 02 bd 02</b>
            <b>39 00 09 d8 e2 aa 03 f0 e2 aa 03 f0</b>
            <b>15 00 02 bd 00</b>
            <b>39 00 03 b6 8d 8d</b>
            <b>15 00 02 cc 04</b>
            <b>39 00 03 c6 ff f9</b>
            <b>39 00 37 e0 00 12 1f 1a 40 4a …</b>          <i>55 bytes — the gamma table</i>
            <b>05 01 01 11</b>                              <i>exit sleep, then 1 ms</i>
            <b>05 01 01 29</b>                              <i>display on, then 1 ms</i>
        <b>];</b>

        display-timings {
            timing-0 {
                <b>clock-frequency = &lt;39600000&gt;;</b>
                <b>hactive = &lt;568&gt;;   vactive = &lt;1232&gt;;</b>
                <b>hfront-porch = &lt;40&gt;;  hback-porch = &lt;140&gt;;  hsync-len = &lt;40&gt;;</b>
                <b>vfront-porch = &lt;16&gt;;  vback-porch = &lt;16&gt;;   vsync-len = &lt;4&gt;;</b>
            };
        };

        <b>ports {</b>                     <i>// the other half of the endpoint pair</i>
            <b>#address-cells = &lt;1&gt;;  #size-cells = &lt;0&gt;;</b>
            <b>port@0 {</b>
                <b>reg = &lt;0&gt;;</b>
                <b>rm69a10_in: endpoint { remote-endpoint = &lt;&amp;dsi_out_rm69a10&gt;; };</b>
            <b>};</b>
        <b>};</b>
    <b>};</b>
};

<em>// NOT HERE, deliberately: no reset-gpios and no backlight GPIO on the panel
//   node. The board .dts attaches both to the 'lcd' label, which is how the
//   reference splits panel from board.</em>`;
---

<HardwareNote
  slug="panel-contract"
  title="A panel is a byte string and eleven numbers"
  eyebrow="panel-canaan-universal.c · hardware note"
  dek="The Canaan DRM panel driver has no knowledge of any display. Everything that makes an ST7701 an ST7701 lives in a 97-line <code>.dtsi</code>, so the RM69A10 is a new file rather than a new driver &mdash; provided its numbers fit the same slots. Two of them do not."
  description="Property by property, what the canaan,universal panel driver reads out of a device tree, how its panel-init-sequence blob is framed, and the 20-command sequence the RM69A10 actually needs."
  source="nix/dts/display-rm69a10-568x1232.dtsi"
  meta={[
    { label: "Driver", value: "panel-canaan-universal.c<br>395 lines, no panel-specific code" },
    { label: "Init sequence", value: `${initSeq.length} commands<br>${payloadTotal} payload bytes` },
    { label: "As a property", value: `${propertyBytes} bytes<br>${payloadTotal} payload + ${initSeq.length}×3 framing` },
    { label: "Timing", value: "39.6 MHz over 788×1268<br>≈ 39.6 Hz, 2 lanes" },
    { label: "Status", value: "compiles and round-trips<br>never lit a panel" },
  ]}
>
  <HwSection kind="the contract" title="Every property the driver reads">
    <p class="hw-lede">
      Where it reads each one, and what it becomes. The two rows at the bottom are
      the interesting ones: the settings the driver does <em>not</em> read, because
      it hardcodes them.
    </p>

    <div class="hw-contract">
      <div class="hw-crow hw-crow-head">
        <span>Device-tree property</span><span>Read at</span><span>Becomes</span>
      </div>

      <div class="hw-crow">
        <span class="a">compatible = "canaan,universal"</span>
        <span class="b">canaan_panel_of_match[]</span>
        <span class="c">
          The only string this driver matches. Panel identity is not encoded
          anywhere &mdash; every Canaan panel is &ldquo;universal&rdquo;.
        </span>
      </div>
      <div class="hw-crow">
        <span class="a">reg = &lt;0&gt;</span>
        <span class="b">the MIPI DSI core</span>
        <span class="c">The DSI virtual channel. Always 0 for a single panel.</span>
      </div>
      <div class="hw-crow">
        <span class="a">panel-dsi-lane</span>
        <span class="b">parse_dt → ctx-&gt;lan_num<br />probe → dsi-&gt;lanes</span>
        <span class="c">
          How many DSI data lanes the host is told to use: 2 for the ST7701, and 2
          for the RM69A10. Note the property name &mdash; the driver&rsquo;s internal
          field is <code>lan_num</code>, but the binding is
          <code>panel-dsi-lane</code>, and a fragment that writes the field name
          instead compiles and is silently ignored.
        </span>
      </div>
      <div class="hw-crow">
        <span class="a">panel-width-mm<br />panel-height-mm</span>
        <span class="b">parse_dt → ctx-&gt;width_mm<br />get_modes → display_info</span>
        <span class="c">
          Physical size in millimetres, which is what DRM and every userspace DPI
          calculation assume. The reference file puts <strong>480 and 800</strong> in
          these fields &mdash; the pixel counts. Ours puts 44 and 95.
        </span>
      </div>
      <div class="hw-crow">
        <span class="a">display-timings &#123; timing-0 &#125;</span>
        <span class="b">of_get_videomode(np, &amp;ctx-&gt;vm, 0)</span>
        <span class="c">
          The standard <code>videomode</code> binding: clock-frequency, hactive,
          vactive and the six porch and sync values, converted straight into a
          <code>drm_display_mode</code> and flagged PREFERRED.
        </span>
      </div>
      <div class="hw-crow">
        <span class="a">panel-init-sequence = [ … ]</span>
        <span class="b">of_get_property +<br />panel_simple_parse_cmd_seq</span>
        <span class="c">
          A raw blob of length-prefixed DCS commands. This is the entire panel
          personality, and for this panel it is 297 bytes of it.
        </span>
      </div>
      <div class="hw-crow">
        <span class="a">dsi_reset-gpios</span>
        <span class="b">devm_gpiod_get(dev, "dsi_reset")</span>
        <span class="c">
          Note the consumer string: <code>dsi_reset</code>, not the conventional
          <code>reset</code>. Supplied by the <em>board</em> file, not the panel
          fragment. On this board it is GPIO22, active high.
        </span>
      </div>
      <div class="hw-crow">
        <span class="a">backlight_gpio-gpios</span>
        <span class="b">devm_gpiod_get(dev, "backlight_gpio")</span>
        <span class="c">
          Stored in a field called <code>power_on</code> and driven high once at
          probe. A panel-enable line, not a brightness control &mdash; there is no PWM
          and no backlight node. GPIO25, active high, also from the board file.
        </span>
      </div>
      <div class="hw-crow">
        <span class="a">ports/port@0/endpoint</span>
        <span class="b">DRM of_graph</span>
        <span class="c">
          Pairs with <code>&amp;dsi</code> port@1. Both halves must name each other or
          the bridge chain never forms and nothing probes.
        </span>
      </div>
      <div class="hw-crow">
        <span class="a">&amp;vo &#123; vth_line = &lt;9&gt;; &#125;</span>
        <span class="b">canaan,k230-vo — not the panel driver</span>
        <span class="c">
          The scanline at which the video output raises its tearing interrupt. It
          lives in the panel fragment because it is a per-panel number, but it
          overrides a property on a different node.
        </span>
      </div>

      <div class="hw-crow hw-crow-hard">
        <span class="a">— not from the device tree —</span>
        <span class="b">probe: dsi-&gt;mode_flags =<br />MIPI_DSI_MODE_VIDEO_SYNC_PULSE</span>
        <span class="c">
          <strong>Hardcoded</strong>, for every panel. The vendor&rsquo;s own RT-Smart
          driver runs the RM69A10 in <code>K_BURST_MODE</code>. No device-tree property
          can change this.
        </span>
      </div>
      <div class="hw-crow hw-crow-hard">
        <span class="a">— not from the device tree —</span>
        <span class="b">probe: dsi-&gt;format =<br />MIPI_DSI_FMT_RGB888</span>
        <span class="c">
          <strong>Hardcoded.</strong> 24 bits per pixel on the wire, regardless of what
          the init sequence sets on the panel side.
        </span>
      </div>
    </div>
  </HwSection>

  <HwSection kind="the blob" title="Reading the init sequence">
    <p class="hw-lede">
      The blob is a flat stream of three-byte headers, each followed by its payload.
      No terminator and no count: the parser walks it twice, once to count commands
      and once to build them, and rejects the whole property if the lengths do not
      land exactly on the end.
    </p>

    <HwFigure
      caption="Wire-level detail the parser hides: <code>panel_simple_xfer_dsi_cmd_seq</code> calls <code>mipi_dsi_dcs_write_buffer</code> for all three accepted <code>data_type</code> values, and that helper picks the real DSI packet type from the buffer length itself. <strong>The type byte is therefore decorative.</strong> The reference blob uses <code>0x39</code> even for single-byte commands, where <code>0x05</code> would be correct, and it works; ours uses the correct type for each length anyway, because a file that is right for the right reason is cheaper to read later."
    >
      <svg
        viewBox="0 0 920 330"
        role="img"
        aria-label="One command in panel-init-sequence: a three-byte header of data type, delay in milliseconds and payload length, followed by exactly payload-length bytes. The example is 39 00 04 b9 ff 83 99."
      >
        <defs>
          <marker id="pb-a" viewBox="0 0 10 10" refX="9" refY="5" markerWidth="7" markerHeight="7" orient="auto-start-reverse">
            <path d="M0,0 L10,5 L0,10 z" fill="var(--ink-2)"></path>
          </marker>
        </defs>

        <text x="8" y="16" font-size="10.5" font-weight="700" letter-spacing="1.1" fill="var(--muted)" font-family="var(--sans)">ONE COMMAND — HEADER, THEN PAYLOAD</text>

        <rect x="8" y="30" width="52" height="40" rx="3" fill="var(--soc-f)" stroke="var(--soc)" stroke-width="1.3"></rect>
        <text x="34" y="56" text-anchor="middle" font-size="15" font-weight="600" fill="var(--soc)" font-family="var(--mono)">39</text>
        <rect x="64" y="30" width="52" height="40" rx="3" fill="var(--unverified-f)" stroke="var(--unverified)" stroke-width="1.3"></rect>
        <text x="90" y="56" text-anchor="middle" font-size="15" font-weight="600" fill="var(--unverified)" font-family="var(--mono)">00</text>
        <rect x="120" y="30" width="52" height="40" rx="3" fill="var(--ours-f)" stroke="var(--ours)" stroke-width="1.3"></rect>
        <text x="146" y="56" text-anchor="middle" font-size="15" font-weight="600" fill="var(--ours)" font-family="var(--mono)">04</text>
        <rect x="176" y="30" width="52" height="40" rx="3" fill="var(--surface)" stroke="var(--rule)" stroke-width="1.2"></rect>
        <text x="202" y="56" text-anchor="middle" font-size="15" font-weight="600" fill="var(--ink-2)" font-family="var(--mono)">b9</text>
        <rect x="232" y="30" width="52" height="40" rx="3" fill="var(--surface)" stroke="var(--rule)" stroke-width="1.2"></rect>
        <text x="258" y="56" text-anchor="middle" font-size="15" font-weight="600" fill="var(--ink-2)" font-family="var(--mono)">ff</text>
        <rect x="288" y="30" width="52" height="40" rx="3" fill="var(--surface)" stroke="var(--rule)" stroke-width="1.2"></rect>
        <text x="314" y="56" text-anchor="middle" font-size="15" font-weight="600" fill="var(--ink-2)" font-family="var(--mono)">83</text>
        <rect x="344" y="30" width="52" height="40" rx="3" fill="var(--surface)" stroke="var(--rule)" stroke-width="1.2"></rect>
        <text x="370" y="56" text-anchor="middle" font-size="15" font-weight="600" fill="var(--ink-2)" font-family="var(--mono)">99</text>

        <line x1="400" y1="50" x2="418" y2="50" stroke="var(--ink-2)" stroke-width="1.3" marker-end="url(#pb-a)"></line>
        <text x="424" y="46" font-size="12.5" fill="var(--muted)" font-family="var(--sans)">the next header starts here — there is no</text>
        <text x="424" y="62" font-size="12.5" fill="var(--muted)" font-family="var(--sans)">separator and no total count anywhere.</text>

        <path d="M8,76 L8,86 L172,86 L172,76" fill="none" stroke="var(--ink-2)" stroke-width="1.1"></path>
        <text x="90" y="102" text-anchor="middle" font-size="12" font-weight="600" fill="var(--ink-2)" font-family="var(--sans)">header — 3 bytes</text>
        <path d="M176,76 L176,86 L396,86 L396,76" fill="none" stroke="var(--ink-2)" stroke-width="1.1"></path>
        <text x="286" y="102" text-anchor="middle" font-size="12" font-weight="600" fill="var(--ink-2)" font-family="var(--sans)">payload — exactly payload_length bytes</text>

        <line x1="34" y1="118" x2="34" y2="132" stroke="var(--soc)" stroke-width="1.2"></line>
        <text x="8" y="150" font-size="12" font-weight="600" fill="var(--soc)" font-family="var(--mono)">data_type</text>
        <text x="8" y="168" font-size="12" fill="var(--ink-2)" font-family="var(--sans)">0x05, 0x15 or 0x39. All</text>
        <text x="8" y="184" font-size="12" fill="var(--ink-2)" font-family="var(--sans)">three are handled the same</text>
        <text x="8" y="200" font-size="12" fill="var(--ink-2)" font-family="var(--sans)">way; anything else aborts</text>
        <text x="8" y="216" font-size="12" fill="var(--ink-2)" font-family="var(--sans)">the probe.</text>

        <line x1="330" y1="118" x2="330" y2="132" stroke="var(--unverified)" stroke-width="1.2"></line>
        <text x="304" y="150" font-size="12" font-weight="600" fill="var(--unverified)" font-family="var(--mono)">delay</text>
        <text x="304" y="168" font-size="12" fill="var(--ink-2)" font-family="var(--sans)">Milliseconds to sleep AFTER</text>
        <text x="304" y="184" font-size="12" fill="var(--ink-2)" font-family="var(--sans)">the command. A u8, so 255 ms</text>
        <text x="304" y="200" font-size="12" fill="var(--ink-2)" font-family="var(--sans)">is the longest wait, and 1 ms</text>
        <text x="304" y="216" font-size="12" fill="var(--ink-2)" font-family="var(--sans)">the shortest non-zero one.</text>

        <line x1="640" y1="118" x2="640" y2="132" stroke="var(--ours)" stroke-width="1.2"></line>
        <text x="614" y="150" font-size="12" font-weight="600" fill="var(--ours)" font-family="var(--mono)">payload_length</text>
        <text x="614" y="168" font-size="12" fill="var(--ink-2)" font-family="var(--sans)">Includes the DCS command byte</text>
        <text x="614" y="184" font-size="12" fill="var(--ink-2)" font-family="var(--sans)">itself, so a bare command with</text>
        <text x="614" y="200" font-size="12" fill="var(--ink-2)" font-family="var(--sans)">no parameters is length 1,</text>
        <text x="614" y="216" font-size="12" fill="var(--ink-2)" font-family="var(--sans)">not 0.</text>

        <line x1="8" y1="240" x2="912" y2="240" stroke="var(--rule)" stroke-width="1"></line>

        <rect x="8" y="258" width="52" height="40" rx="3" fill="var(--soc-f)" stroke="var(--soc)" stroke-width="1.3"></rect>
        <text x="34" y="284" text-anchor="middle" font-size="15" font-weight="600" fill="var(--soc)" font-family="var(--mono)">05</text>
        <rect x="64" y="258" width="52" height="40" rx="3" fill="var(--unverified-f)" stroke="var(--unverified)" stroke-width="1.3"></rect>
        <text x="90" y="284" text-anchor="middle" font-size="15" font-weight="600" fill="var(--unverified)" font-family="var(--mono)">01</text>
        <rect x="120" y="258" width="52" height="40" rx="3" fill="var(--ours-f)" stroke="var(--ours)" stroke-width="1.3"></rect>
        <text x="146" y="284" text-anchor="middle" font-size="15" font-weight="600" fill="var(--ours)" font-family="var(--mono)">01</text>
        <rect x="176" y="258" width="52" height="40" rx="3" fill="var(--surface)" stroke="var(--rule)" stroke-width="1.2"></rect>
        <text x="202" y="284" text-anchor="middle" font-size="15" font-weight="600" fill="var(--ink-2)" font-family="var(--mono)">11</text>
        <text x="242" y="276" font-size="13" font-weight="600" fill="var(--ink)" font-family="var(--sans)">DCS 0x11 — exit_sleep_mode, then wait 1 ms</text>
        <text x="242" y="294" font-size="12.5" fill="var(--muted)" font-family="var(--sans)">the vendor waits 300 microseconds here, which this field cannot express</text>
      </svg>
    </HwFigure>

    <HwNote kind="undeclared" label="A correction: this sequence is 20 commands, not 13">
      <p>
        An earlier transcription of this note recorded the RM69A10 init sequence as
        &ldquo;13 commands, 75 bytes&rdquo;, and listed a page-select and
        window-setting sequence that does not appear in
        <code>rm69a10_568x1232_dsi_send()</code> at all. The real sequence is
        <strong>20 commands and 297 payload bytes</strong>, 357 bytes as a device-tree
        property once the framing is counted. Seven commands were missing, including
        the two largest.
      </p>
      <p>
        The authoritative transcription is in the repository, with a source line
        recorded per command, and the committed <code>.dtsi</code> matches it byte for
        byte. The earlier claim that &ldquo;the AMOLED is far simpler&rdquo; than the
        ST7701 followed from the wrong number and is also wrong: it has fewer
        commands, and more payload.
      </p>
    </HwNote>

    <div class="hw-strip" role="img" aria-label={`The twenty commands of the RM69A10 init sequence drawn in proportion to payload length, totalling ${payloadTotal} bytes; the 45-byte 0xB4 block and the 55-byte 0xE0 gamma table together are a third of it.`}>
      {
        initSeq.map((c) => (
          <span
            class:list={[c.bytes >= 40 && "heavy"]}
            style={`flex:${c.bytes} 1 0`}
            title={`0x${c.cmd} — ${c.what} — ${c.bytes} bytes`}
          />
        ))
      }
    </div>
    <p class="hw-strip-scale">
      <span>20 commands, in order, drawn in proportion to payload</span>
      <span>0xB4 · 45 B and 0xE0 · 55 B in violet — a third of the blob</span>
    </p>

    <p class="hw-p">
      The vendor also issues two DCS <em>reads</em> after the sequence, of the error
      register and the pixel format, apparently for debugging. The
      <code>panel-init-sequence</code> binding is write-only, so there is nowhere to
      put them. One further command, a solid blue test pattern, is sent only when the
      vendor&rsquo;s <code>test_mode_en</code> is set, and is not part of normal
      initialisation.
    </p>
  </HwSection>

  <HwSection kind="lifecycle" title="When the reset line actually moves">
    <p class="hw-lede">
      This is the part that will cost someone an afternoon. The reset pulse happens
      in <code>probe</code>, once. The reset code inside <code>prepare</code> &mdash;
      the callback DRM invokes every time the panel comes back &mdash; is present in
      the source and entirely commented out.
    </p>

    <HwFigure
