  <HwSection kind="comparison" title="The two panels, number for number">
    <p class="hw-lede">
      Left column is what the reference <code>.dtsi</code> contains. Right column is
      what the vendor&rsquo;s connector table says about the RM69A10 &mdash; the same
      numbers the shipped RT-Smart firmware runs on, which makes them observations of
      working firmware rather than datasheet claims.
    </p>

    <div class="hw-table">
      <table>
        <thead>
          <tr><th>Property</th><th>ST7701 — the reference</th><th>RM69A10 — ours</th><th>Note</th></tr>
        </thead>
        <tbody>
          <tr><th>Active pixels</th><td>480 × 800</td><td class="ours">568 × 1232</td><td class="remark">portrait either way</td></tr>
          <tr><th>Panel type</th><td>RGB LCD</td><td class="ours">AMOLED</td><td class="remark">no backlight to dim</td></tr>
          <tr><th>clock-frequency</th><td>39 600 000</td><td class="ours">39 600 000</td><td class="remark">identical, coincidentally</td></tr>
          <tr><th>hsync / hbp / hfp</th><td>20 / 20 / 80</td><td class="ours">40 / 140 / 40</td><td class="remark">htotal 600 against 788</td></tr>
          <tr><th>vsync / vbp / vfp</th><td>10 / 70 / 220</td><td class="ours">4 / 16 / 16</td><td class="remark">vtotal 1100 against 1268</td></tr>
          <tr><th>Refresh, computed</th><td>60.0 Hz</td><td class="ours">39.6 Hz</td><td class="remark">pclk ÷ (htotal × vtotal)</td></tr>
          <tr><th>DSI lanes</th><td>2</td><td class="ours">2</td><td class="remark">—</td></tr>
          <tr><th>Per-lane rate</th><td>475.2 Mbps</td><td class="ours">475.2 Mbps</td><td class="remark">matches the vendor phyclk exactly</td></tr>
          <tr><th>DSI video mode</th><td>sync-pulse, from the driver</td><td class="ours">K_BURST_MODE, per the vendor</td><td class="remark">the driver cannot express burst</td></tr>
          <tr><th>vth_line</th><td>10</td><td class="ours">9</td><td class="remark">from the connector table</td></tr>
          <tr><th>Init commands</th><td>44</td><td class="ours">20</td><td class="remark">fewer commands</td></tr>
          <tr><th>Init payload</th><td>237 bytes</td><td class="ours">297 bytes</td><td class="remark">and more payload — the AMOLED is not simpler</td></tr>
          <tr><th>As a property</th><td>369 bytes</td><td class="ours">357 bytes</td><td class="remark">payload plus three framing bytes per command</td></tr>
          <tr><th>Pixel format sent</th><td>0x3A 0x50 → 16 bpp</td><td class="ours">not sent</td><td class="remark">the driver forces RGB888 regardless</td></tr>
          <tr><th>panel-width-mm</th><td>480 — a pixel count</td><td class="ours">44</td><td class="remark">we diverged; see below</td></tr>
          <tr><th>Reset GPIO</th><td>IO22, active high</td><td class="ours">IO22, active high</td><td class="remark">same pin, same polarity</td></tr>
          <tr><th>Backlight GPIO</th><td>IO25, active high</td><td class="ours">IO25, active high</td><td class="remark">same pin, same polarity</td></tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>

    <HwNote kind="unverified" label="39.6 Hz, despite the name">
      <p>
        The vendor&rsquo;s enum is <code>RM69A10_MIPI_2LAN_568X1232_60FPS</code>, and
        the timing row next to it is
        <code>&#123; 39600, 475200, 788, 568, 40, 140, 40, 1268, 1232, 4, 16, 16 &#125;</code>.
        39.6 MHz over a 788 × 1268 total frame is 39.63 Hz. The numbers are internally
        consistent &mdash; 39.6 MHz × 24 bpp ÷ 2 lanes is exactly the 475 200 kHz PHY
        clock in the same row &mdash; so the pixel clock is real and the name is wrong.
      </p>
      <p>
        60 Hz would need roughly 60 MHz and a 720 Mbps lane rate. Whether the panel or
        the K230&rsquo;s DSI PHY will take that is an open question; the shipped
        firmware does not try.
      </p>
    </HwNote>

    <HwNote kind="ours" label="We put real millimetres in, and the reference does not">
      <p>
        The reference writes <code>panel-width-mm = &lt;480&gt;</code> and
        <code>panel-height-mm = &lt;800&gt;</code> for a panel whose pixel count is
        480 × 800. A 480 mm display is half a metre across. The driver hands these
        straight to <code>connector-&gt;display_info.width_mm</code>, so anything
        computing DPI gets 25.4 dpi.
      </p>
      <p>
        Our fragment writes 44 and 95, computed from the stated 4.1&Prime; diagonal at
        this aspect ratio. That is arithmetic, not a measurement, and it is a
        deliberate divergence from the reference rather than an oversight: being
        bug-compatible with the vendor buys nothing that a correct number does not.
      </p>
    </HwNote>
  </HwSection>

  <HwSection kind="the real thing" title="The fragment we wrote">
    <p class="hw-lede">
      An earlier draft of this note carried a fragment that compiled and was
      structurally wrong: the panel sat at the root of the tree rather than inside
      <code>&amp;dsi</code>, the lane count used the driver&rsquo;s internal field name
      instead of the binding, the panel node carried its own
      <code>reset-gpios</code>, and both the endpoint graph and
      <code>&amp;vo &#123; vth_line &#125;</code> were missing. Compiling in isolation proved
      the syntax parsed; it said nothing about whether the driver would ever see the
      node. This is the corrected file.
    </p>

    <pre class="hw-code" set:html={dtsi} />

    <HwNote kind="ours" label="Four structural corrections, spelled out">
      <p>
        The panel is a DSI child &mdash;
        <code>&amp;dsi &#123; lcd: panel@0 &#123; reg = &lt;0&gt;; … &#125; &#125;</code>
        &mdash; not a root node. The lane property is <code>panel-dsi-lane</code>, not
        <code>lan-num</code>. The panel node carries no GPIOs at all: reset and
        backlight are attached by the board <code>.dts</code> to the <code>lcd</code>
        label, which is the whole reason that label exists. And the fragment needs both
        the ports/endpoint graph back to <code>&amp;dsi</code> port@1 and
        <code>&amp;vo &#123; vth_line = &lt;9&gt;; &#125;</code>.
      </p>
      <p>
        After the rewrite the only structural difference from the reference fragment is
        the label names. The device tree round-trips: 357 bytes of init sequence,
        39.6 MHz, 568×1232, two lanes, <code>vth_line</code> 9, endpoint linked.
      </p>
    </HwNote>

    <HwNote kind="unverified" label="Still unverified: that any of this lights the panel">
      <p>
        Compiling proves the description is well-formed, not that it is correct. The
        init sequence is transcribed from working vendor code, but the timings, the
        reset polarity and the burst-mode mismatch are all untested on hardware, and
        Linux has not booted on this board.
      </p>
    </HwNote>
  </HwSection>

  <HwSection kind="open" title="What still has to be settled">
    <div class="hw-q">
      <div>
        <span class="num">1</span>
        <div>
          <h3>Sync-pulse video mode against burst</h3>
          <p>
            The driver hardcodes <code>MIPI_DSI_MODE_VIDEO_SYNC_PULSE</code>; the vendor
            runs this panel in burst mode, and the porches in the timing row were chosen
            for burst. Either the RM69A10 tolerates sync-pulse at these timings, or
            <code>panel-canaan-universal.c</code> needs a <code>mode_flags</code>
            property adding. There is no way to find out except to try it.
          </p>
        </div>
      </div>
      <div>
        <span class="num">2</span>
        <div>
          <h3>Whether prepare needs fixing before anything works at all</h3>
          <p>
            A first boot might well come up, because <code>probe</code> does pulse reset.
            The question is whether DRM calls <code>unprepare</code> at any point during
            normal bring-up &mdash; a mode negotiation, a console switch &mdash; before
            the first frame. If it does, the panel is in reset by the time
            <code>prepare</code> runs and nothing will appear. Uncommenting the reset
            block is a four-line patch; it may be a prerequisite rather than a polish
            item.
          </p>
        </div>
      </div>
      <div>
        <span class="num">3</span>
        <div>
          <h3>What IO25 actually gates</h3>
          <p>
            The reference board file settles the easy half: GPIO25, active high,
            consumed as <code>backlight_gpio</code>. The U-Boot pinmux calls it
            <code>LCD_EN</code> and the RT-Smart config calls it
            <code>MPP_DSI_LCD_BACKLIGHT_PIN</code>. An AMOLED has no backlight, so it is
            presumably a panel power enable &mdash; and if it gates a rail, the
            driver&rsquo;s habit of dropping it in <code>unprepare</code> and never
            raising it again matters more, not less. The schematic around that net has
            not been read.
          </p>
        </div>
      </div>
      <div>
        <span class="num">4</span>
        <div>
          <h3>Brightness, and the delays the binding cannot express</h3>
          <p>
            An AMOLED&rsquo;s brightness is a DCS write, which this binding can only do
            at init, so a real control means either a backlight device in the driver or a
            userspace path that writes DCS directly. Separately, the vendor&rsquo;s two
            post-command delays are 300 and 100 <em>micro</em>seconds and the binding&rsquo;s
            field is a u8 of milliseconds; both become 1 ms, which is longer than the
            vendor waits. Worth watching if the panel misbehaves at init.
          </p>
        </div>
      </div>
      <div>
        <span class="num">5</span>
        <div>
          <h3>Rotation and mirroring</h3>
          <p>
            568 × 1232 is a phone-shaped portrait panel. Nothing in this binding carries a
            <code>rotation</code> property, and the vendor&rsquo;s mirror control is a
            runtime patch of the <code>0xCC</code> parameter that a static byte string
            cannot express &mdash; ours carries <code>0x04</code>, the unmirrored default.
            Whatever orientation the panel comes up in is what DRM will report.
          </p>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </HwSection>

  <div class="hw-sources">
    <p>
      Driver: drivers/gpu/drm/panel/panel-canaan-universal.c. Reference fragment:
      arch/riscv/boot/dts/canaan/display-st7701-480x800.dtsi. Both at
      ruyisdk/linux-xuantie-kernel 7d4e1f444f461dbe3833bd99a4640e7b6c2cd529; the byte
      and command counts for both panels are from parsing the two blobs, not from the
      files' own comments.
    </p>
    <p>
      RM69A10 values: mpp/kernel/connector/src/rm69a10.c and connector_info_list[] in
      mpp/userapps/src/connector/mpi_connector.c, plus MPP_DSI_LCD_RESET_PIN=22 and
      BACKLIGHT_PIN=25 from the vendor's auto.conf. The per-command transcription with
      a source line each is committed in this repository.
    </p>
    <p>
      Refresh rates, lane rates and physical dimensions here are arithmetic on those
      values, not measurements. Nothing on this page has lit a panel.
    </p>
  </div>
</HardwareNote>
